Directive 05, part 1 of 2 (2026-10-14). SPEC v0.10 and the shared-dictionary
codec, tests first. 224 tests pass. Determinism holds.

DONE: scope 1 (SPEC v0.10, additive), scope 2 (codec in wax-core), scope 5
(the tests, written before the implementation).

NOT DONE, and deliberately not rushed: scope 3 (builder carry-forward wiring --
the wax-core API exists and is tested, but wax-builder does not yet call it on
append), scope 4 (zim2wax emitting v0.10), scope 6 (re-measurement), scope 7
(ADR, externally blocked), scope 8 (converter cleanup).

Why I stopped where I did: scope 4 needs zim2wax to collect training samples
before it writes, which means touching the single streaming pass that is A2e's
memory-bounded property -- the thing Directive 01 measured and the thing a
hasty refactor would quietly break. Scope 6 cannot be honestly reported without
scope 4. The directive says not to claim the win without re-measured numbers,
so the win is not claimed.

Three things found by measuring rather than assuming:

1. Dictionary training is deterministic but NOT order-independent. Five rounds
   on identical input gave identical output; reversing sample order gave a
   different dictionary. SPEC 5.9 therefore REQUIRES content-derived sample
   ordering. Had this gone unchecked it would have been a silent
   reproducibility failure that only showed up across machines.

2. The dictionary belongs in the index segment for a security reason, not a
   convenience one. The signing digest is SHA-256(header + index-chain), so the
   blob region is outside the signature. Every dict-framed entry depends on the
   dictionary, so an unsigned one lets an attacker change all decoded content
   with the signature still verifying.

3. The red state was exactly four missing API items -- no accidental surface.
   That is what TDD bought here.

